[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59255,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Soft tissue chondroma is a benign neoplasm composed of chondrocytes that produce hyaline or myxoid cartilage that arises in the extraosseous and extrasynovial soft tissues.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59256,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "<p>9220/0 Chondroma NOS</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-31T09:36:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59257,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>2E89.1 &amp; XH0NS4 Benign tumours of uncertain differentiation, soft tissue &amp; Chondroma NOS</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T17:08:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59258,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> chondroma of soft parts; extraskeletal chondroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59259,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chondroblastoma-like soft tissue chondroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59260,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately 80% of cases occur in the region of the fingers {26297064; 30033358}. The remainder of cases arise most commonly in the hands, followed by the toes and feet, with origin in the trunk and the head and neck region {19349148} being uncommon. Rare examples have been described in the skin {25998426}, lip {29115679}, dura {25250651}, and tongue {30845089}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59261,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is solitary and presents as a painless, firm, slowly enlarging mass {26297064}. Radiographically, it is well demarcated, is separated from bone, and has spiculated or ring-like calcifications. The cartilage is bright on T2-weighted MRI.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59262,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients are usually middle-aged (average: 34.5&nbsp;years), and males are affected more frequently than females (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1.5:1) {76505}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59263,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59264,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytogenetic analyses of limited numbers of soft tissue chondromas reveal several different aberrations, including rearrangements of 12q13-q15 and trisomy&nbsp;5, and in a set of tumours with rearrangements of 12q13-q15, a truncated or full-length HMGA2 transcript was found {8402563; 10748295; 11793371; 14614053; 25250651}. In about 50% of the cases, <em>FN1</em> gene rearrangements were reported, with <em>FGFR1</em> and <em>FGFR2</em> as fusion partners {31273315}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59265,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Soft tissue chondroma is a well-circumscribed, nodular, solid mass that is greyish-bluish-white and sometimes focally myxoid. Most tumours are solid and 1&ndash;2&nbsp;cm in size.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59266,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour is composed of lobules of well-formed hyaline and/or myxoid cartilage that are delineated by fibrous septa {76505}. The cartilage is often hypercellular and the chondrocytes are arranged in clusters and reside within lacunar spaces or float in a flocculent myxoid matrix. The nuclei are usually small, round, and dark, but they may be large with fine or coarse chromatin and small nucleoli and exhibit mild to moderate pleomorphism. Coarse calcifications and endochondral ossification may be present. The chondrocytes in the mineralized areas undergo necrosis, and heavily calcified tumours may have an infiltrate of histiocytes that may obscure the nature of the lesion. The chondroblastoma-like subtype is hypercellular and contains chondrocytes with nuclei that are often grooved or cleaved and have moderate amounts of eosinophilic cytoplasm; the matrix contains calcifications that surround individual chondrocytes, thereby mimicking chondroblastoma {11342780}. Regardless of the morphology, mitotic activity is limited and abnormal mitotic figures are not observed.</p>\r\n<h5><em>Differential diagnosis</em></h5>\r\n<p>The tumour is distinguished from synovial chondromatosis because it lacks an association with synovium. It is separated from juxtacortical chondroma because it lacks attachment to bone. Soft tissue chondrosarcoma exhibits greater cellularity, cytological atypia, and mitotic atypia.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T12:05:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59267,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59268,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59269,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a soft tissue mass composed of nodules of well-delineated cartilage; matrix is hyaline or myxoid and may calcify; chondrocytes show limited atypia and little mitotic activity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59270,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59271,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 96,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Treatment is marginal excision, and the recurrence rate is 15&ndash;20% {76505}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:59:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]